\par
As Milton looked back on the startling changes of his age \f1\emdash  the dismantling of the state religion, the execution of a king, the abolition of monarchy, reconquest of Ireland, rule by a republic \emdash  he knew he had not been an idle witness, but an active participant. The author of England\rquote s greatest epic poem had early on decided to devote himself to a life of poetry, but in the civil war period Milton became a political advocate. He felt that God had chosen him, and Protestant England, for a special purpose. Considering himself \lquote Tireless\'85for the Sake of Liberty\rquote , Milton risked punishment by attacking state and church censorship in the publishing industry, believing that to print books was \lquote a privilege of the people\rquote  (Areopagitica). Seeing there were no laws for divorce in England at that time, he published controversial proposals to legalise divorce (Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce, Tetrachordon). Resisting compulsion, hierarchy and uniformity in the Church, he advocated a broad toleration within Reformed Christianity and also suggested full-scale educational reform of the young (Of Education). Most courageously, he wrote pamphlets justifying the execution of King Charles I (Tenure of Kings and Magistrates, Eikonoklastes, First and Second Defenses of the English People), and he defended republicanism, the system of government without kings (The Readie and Easy Way to Establish a Free Commonwealth). These earned Milton the epithet \lquote king-killer\rquote , and he was taunted after going blind by those who saw his blindness as God\rquote s punishment for his views. \par
\par
Some of this writing Milton was hired to do, after his appointment as a servant of the Cromwellian government, but these works voice his deep principles. Milton believed that \lquote all men naturally were born free\rquote  (Tenure of Kings and Magistrates), and that a rigorous pursuit of virtue, along with freedom of conscience, would create the best society. This was the principle behind his commitment to freedoms of the press: \par
\par
So truth be in the field, we do injuriously by licensing and prohibiting to misdoubt her strength. Let her and Falsehood grapple; who ever knew Truth put to the worse, in a free and open encounter. (Areopagitica) \par
\par
Milton was no democrat or freethinker, however, and on two issues his principles outpaced his politics. On Ireland, for example, he supported English colonisation; and on Roman Catholicism, he was intolerant, seeing those who adhered to the supremacy of the Papacy as subversive of the state. By 1652, at the age of 43, he was completely blind. After the return of monarchy in 1660, Milton\rquote s republican writings were condemned to be burnt, and the author was sent to prison. There he worked on his Paradise Lost, and after his release, published little political prose ever again. \par
\par
Epic and politics \par
\par
Was Milton\rquote s poetry of a piece with his political writing? Biography is one thing \emdash  poetry is, of course, another. But Paradise Lost is not just any poetry, it is epic poetry. This literary genre had long been the vehicle for philosophical and political speculation. In taking up epic, Milton adopted the most prestigious and ambitious literary form of his age, choosing a mode that encompassed a variety of literary forms and through which his great predecessors, including Homer, Virgil, Dante, and most recently Spenser in English, had conducted their political analysis of government, ethics and society. He thus initiated a conversation with his predecessors over such topics. \par
\par
First in this conversation was Virgil\rquote s Aeneid, so called because of the name of its hero, Aeneas, who fulfils his divinely ordained destiny to found a great empire. A Latin epic poem in 12 books of hexameter verse, the Aeneid celebrated the prehistory and origins of the Roman Empire, leading to the line of the Caesars. On the opposite side of the political spectrum was Lucan\rquote s Pharsalia, another of Milton\rquote s influences and a Latin epic that gave a negative rewriting of the Aeneid. In its ten books of hexameter verse, Lucan\rquote s Pharsalia pictured the Roman civil war, the downfall of the Roman republic, and the loss of liberty with Caesar\rquote s triumph. Lucan detested Caesar, but could admire his greatness. These two conflicting attitudes towards political achievement, heroism and liberty form key literary and ideological tensions in the poem. \par
\par
Lost causes? \par
\par
With his title, Paradise Lost, was Milton defending his lost cause by writing politics in poetry? Even at the level of prosody, Milton voiced his revolutionary principles. Instead of writing rhymed verse, the standard mode for epic poetry in his day, Milton saw his blank verse as an \lquote ancient liberty recovered to heroic poem from the troublesome and modern bondage of rhyming\rquote . Still, Milton\rquote s poetry does not polemicise the way his prose writings did, and a search for Milton\rquote s \lquote views\rquote  in his poetry will not yield firm answers. Instead, in Paradise Lost we can see how Milton is a poet asking political questions \emdash  indeed, making them fundamental to his poetic endeavour. What if the cause you fought for lost? What if you didn\rquote t know whether you were fighting for the right cause? What if yours was the wrong cause? How could you tell? These were Milton\rquote s worries. \par
\par
In its first books, Paradise Lost poses these questions through the classical values of heroism in relation to true liberty and virtue. Satan defends his rebellion against what he calls God\rquote s \lquote tyranny of heaven\rquote  (l. 124), in much the same language as Milton had used to attack King Charles. Indeed, in the early books of Paradise Lost Milton creates a glorious image of Satan as a heroic freedom-fighter, one who gets the best lines \emdash  \lquote Better to reign in hell, than serve in heaven\rquote  (l. 263), and \lquote The mind is its own place, and in itself / Can make a heaven of hell, a hell of heaven\rquote  (l. 254\endash 5). \par
\par
Fine words like these, albeit an echo of Marlowe\rquote s blasphemous Dr Faustus, make for compelling oratory, all the more so because of Milton\rquote s tight rhetorical patterning. Satan deploys a classic example of the rhetorical figure of antimetabole, where words are repeated and inverted (heaven-hell-hell-heaven) for emphasis and to sound good, almost to replace thought with sound. Such a reversal of the order of repeated words sharpens their sense of unity but also can convey contrast. Or is Satan\rquote s rhetorical prowess also a trap, a circular path to nowhere? Is there something self-serving about his inversion, perhaps fitting for his character as an orator who can make the weaker argument seem the better, and for whom his circular form will culminate in his transformation into a curling serpent? Milton plants landmines like this one throughout his poem, especially in the Satan sections. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Educating the reader \par
\par
Milton\rquote s commitment to freedom of debate was a key component of his political vision, to train readers how to tell the worth of their cause. In Paradise Lost, Milton\rquote s poem asks its readers to test arguments. Milton takes great risks in offering his various points of view. The Romantic poet William Blake found Satan\rquote s allure so great that he claimed Milton was \lquote of the Devil\rquote s party without knowing it\rquote . This kind of strong reading is central to Milton\rquote s political aims of educating readers in discernment and prudence. \par
\par
Milton is a great poet of special effects, and this can make his poetry difficult. T. S. Eliot, for one, found the following sublime description of hell exemplary of Milton\rquote s poverty at physical description: \par
\par
As one great furnace flamed, yet from those flames \par
No light, but rather darkness visible\'85 (l. 62\endash 3). \par
\par
Another of Milton\rquote s landmines is apparent where Satan\rquote s perspective invites readers to experience the tremendous effects of time and space in hell from the perspective of a heroic, even sublime, grandeur and vision \emdash  or so it seems. As Satan first comes to his senses after his violent ejection from heaven, he is racked with psychological more than physical torment: \par
\par
\'85the thought \par
Both of lost happiness and lasting pain \par
Torments him; round he throws his baleful eyes \par
That witnessed huge affliction and dismay \par
Mixed with obdurate pride and steadfast hate\'85 (l. 54\endash 9) \par
\par
This passage does not describe what Satan views, but rather shows how his vision has been changed by experience, and Milton accomplishes this by his secret weapon: his strong adjectives qualifying his nouns. Through these, he heightens intensity and precision. He also teaches readers the meaning of how to qualify, how to make distinctions, how to read between the lines. Satan\rquote s perception contains not just \lquote pride\rquote , but \lquote obdurate\rquote  pride. Here Milton takes his word from the Latin lexicon of durus as \lquote hard\rquote , but in Milton\rquote s Christian vocabulary the term \lquote obdurate\rquote  also evokes a religious sense of hardness of heart, even impenitence. The second adjective qualifying Satan\rquote s perception here is \lquote steadfast\rquote , the meaning of which is \lquote unshaken, immovable in faith, resolution, friendship, etc\rquote  (Oxford English Dictionary, 2). Though we usually think of steadfastness as a positive, heroic quality, the sense of the preceding adjective now qualifies the later one: Satan\rquote s attribute of \lquote steadfastness\rquote  is cast in relief by the thought that it might be a stubborness, or even an impiety. \par
\par
Using the language \par
\par
In the adjectives like those above, Milton asks his readers to ponder the nature of heroic virtue. Even closer attention reveals the twin etymologies of Milton\rquote s poetry: \lquote obdurate\rquote  emerges from the classical sources of his ethical and Christian views (Latin), but \lquote steadfast\rquote  hails from the native traditions of Old and Middle English, as do the adjectives \lquote huge\rquote  and \lquote baleful\rquote , the alliterative pair \lquote waste\rquote  and \lquote wild\rquote , and the Old English verb \lquote ken\rquote  (\lquote knowing\rquote ) in the lines that follow. Milton puts the linguistic resources of his native lexis to work, making use of what was seen as the roughness, or primitiveness of the English. Milton knew ten languages, and wrote in four. His epic has long been attacked for its Latin vocabulary, but his description of Satan\rquote s view of hell is a pairing of words that expresses a complexity of thought, using the roughness of his native or primitive tongue with the elegance and civility of the Latin. As Milton had sworn allegiance to his native language, he was aware of its prestige being lower than that of the ancients. Yet here, it seems the perfect vehicle for that purpose of drawing attention away from the world around Satan and towards the mechanisms of perception through blunt, simple, English language: \par
\par
At once as far as angels\rquote  ken he views \par
The dismal situation waste and wild, \par
A dungeon horrible, on all sides round \par
As one great furnace flamed, yet from those flames \par
No light, but rather darkness visible \par
Served only to discover sights of woe, \par
Regions of sorrow, doleful shades, where peace \par
And rest can never dwell, hope never comes \par
That comes to all\'85 (l. 59\endash 67) \par
\par
It is paramount to the method of Paradise Lost that Satan\rquote s is the consciousness that filters this view. In these lines, Milton alludes to a hell of another poet, Dante, whose Inferno presents an inscription on Hell\rquote s gate: \lquote All hope abandon, you who enter here\rquote  (Inferno iii.9). But in Paradise Lost, the meaning is changed. For Milton\rquote s inhabitants of hell, hope is not theirs to abandon in the first place. The verb tenses shift in this consideration of hope \emdash  from the past tense of narrating Satan\rquote s perspective to an ongoing present tense of eternal time that stretches out to include Milton\rquote s own readers in the future. The striking line break at 66 (\lquote hope never comes/\rquote ) leaves the reader on a precipice of despair, indeterminate until the fall into the following line. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Satan\rquote s perspective is not narrowly psychological in a modern sense, however, based on Satan\rquote s \lquote feelings\rquote , but rather part of a theology of despair. Objective description in hell, then, is impossible; it is consistently moralised. In his representation of hell, Milton condenses anguish, loss, lament and inspiration, a geography of darkness that inspires Satan\rquote s renewal of energy, and tempts readers to partake in his sin of despair, of negating God. These paradoxes of Satan\rquote s vision reflect an inner world bereft of the goodness of order and harmony, judgement and true virtue. It would be the task of Milton\rquote s \lquote fit readers\rquote  to discern these qualities and to cultivate ethical habits and political institutions to ensure their maintenance. The genre of epic, with its representing moral psychology through character and speech, is a means to experience the captivation, and dangers, of political seduction. Milton offers Satan in Paradise Lost as an education for readers in political literacy. \par
\par
